Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 09:56:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 29/31] extensions: answer clipboardRead and clipboardWrite as
 Chrome does

An extension that declares clipboardWrite could not copy without a
gesture, and one that declares clipboardRead was asked before it read.
Blink asks the frame's content settings client before the asynchronous
clipboard requests a permission and before document.execCommand reads
or writes, and ContentSettingsObserverQt left AllowReadFromClipboard()
and AllowWriteToClipboard() at Blink's default, false.

Both now answer from the extension context of the script running, as
chrome/renderer/chrome_content_settings_agent_delegate.cc does: reading
needs clipboardRead, and writing needs clipboardWrite or a privileged
extension page other than a service worker. A password manager's
offscreen document copies a password with execCommand("copy") and no
gesture, and its contents have no view delegate to ask from, so the
answer has to come from the renderer.

anExtensionUsesTheClipboardItsPermissionsAllow checks that a web page
can neither copy nor paste without a gesture, and that an extension that
declares both permissions pastes from its page and copies from its
offscreen document through a selected text area. An offscreen document
is not a privileged page, so its copy needs clipboardWrite. Without this
patch the paste is refused. A copy from the extension's own page writes
with or without the permission, as in Chrome: a second extension that
declares neither copies from its page and is refused a paste.

See villekivela/omaweb#745.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
